---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Laravel Herd (Windows & macOS)"
  path="/dev/laravel-herd"
  section="dev"
  description="Run KoAkademy locally with Herd's native PHP — no Docker required."
>
  <p>
    Herd is a native local PHP environment for Windows and macOS: pre-compiled
    PHP, Nginx, DNS, Composer, Node, and the Laravel installer — no Docker, no
    Homebrew juggling. The free tier covers PHP and the toolchain; PostgreSQL,
    MySQL, and Redis are Herd Pro services. Projects in a parked folder are
    served automatically at <code>*.test</code> domains.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install Herd</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>macOS:</strong> download the <code>.dmg</code>, drag Herd into
      Applications, and complete the onboarding. Herd installs a privileged
      helper for Nginx and Dnsmasq.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windows:</strong> run the installer as administrator. The
      <code>HerdHelper</code> service manages the hosts file and maps parked
      directories to <code>.test</code> domains.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>Confirm PHP 8.5 is available and set it as the default:</p>
  <pre><code>herd use 8.5
php -v</code></pre>
  <p>
    Composer, the Laravel installer, and nvm for Node come bundled. On Windows,
    add an exclusion in Windows Security for the Herd configuration folder to
    keep PHP fast.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Get the code</h2>
  <pre><code>cd ~/Herd
git clone https://github.com/yukazakiri/koakademy.git
cd koakademy
composer install
npm ci
cp .env.example .env
php artisan key:generate</code></pre>
  <p>
    Because <code>~/Herd</code> is a parked folder, the project is immediately
    available at <code>http://koakademy.test</code>. If you clone it anywhere
    else, register it with <code>herd link koakademy</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Database</h2>
  <p>Two options, both fine for local development:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>SQLite (free):</strong>
      <pre><code>touch database/database.sqlite</code></pre>
      In <code>.env</code>, set <code>DB_CONNECTION=sqlite</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>PostgreSQL (Herd Pro):</strong> create and start the service,
      <pre><code>herd services:create postgresql
herd services:start postgresql</code></pre>
      then in <code>.env</code> set <code>DB_CONNECTION=pgsql</code>,
      <code>DB_HOST=127.0.0.1</code>, <code>DB_PORT=5432</code>,
      <code>DB_DATABASE=koakademy</code>, and the username and password Herd
      shows for the service (the default local user is <code>herd</code>).
      Create the <code>koakademy</code> database from the Herd GUI or with the
      <code>herd services</code> CLI commands your version provides.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Working on queues, cache, or sessions? Add Redis the same way
    (<code>herd services:create redis</code>) and point
    <code>REDIS_HOST=127.0.0.1</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Migrate and serve</h2>
  <pre><code>php artisan migrate</code></pre>
  <p>
    Run the Vite dev server for frontend work with <code>npm run dev</code>, or
    build once with <code>npm run build</code>. Open
    <code>http://koakademy.test/setup</code> to create development data and the
    first administrator.
  </p>

  <h2>5. HTTPS and sharing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>herd secure</code> serves the site over a trusted local HTTPS certificate.</li>
    <li><code>herd share</code> publishes a public tunnel URL for webhooks or device testing (backed by Expose).</li>
    <li><code>herd isolate 8.5</code> pins a PHP version per project when the global default differs.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. Validate</h2>
  <pre><code>composer test
vendor/bin/pint --test
npm run build
npm run format:check</code></pre>

  <div class="doc-callout doc-callout-note">
    <p class="doc-callout-label">PDF features need Gotenberg</p>
    <p>
      Herd does not bundle Gotenberg. If you work on PDF generation, run
      <code>docker run -d -p 3000:3000 gotenberg/gotenberg:8</code> and set
      <code>GOTENBERG_URL=http://127.0.0.1:3000</code> in <code>.env</code> — or
      skip PDF work locally.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
